NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
file
- determine the type of a file
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
file [ -f list ] file ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
File attempts to guess the type of each named file from its contents
and prints the name, a tab, and the type, one file per line in the
order the files were named.
.sp
The types it reports are:
.sp
.in +5
directory

empty

character special (major/minor)

block special (major/minor)

executable

executable not stripped

archive

old archive

c program text

fortran program text

assembler program text

roff, nroff, or eqn input text

troff output

commands text

English text

ascii text

data

.in -5
.sp
A file is executable when it begins with the object file magic 0x99,
which is what the linker writes, and a symbol table still present in
it adds the words "not stripped" to that report.
.sp
A file that is text, and not source in any of the languages above, is
commands text when it carries an execute bit and ascii text when it
does not - unless a letter count says it reads like English, in which
case it is English text instead.
.sp
Anything left over that has a byte with its top bit set is data, and
is not looked at further.
.sp
The first four types are read from the inode alone, so such a file is
not opened.  A file that cannot be opened for any other reason is
reported as "cannot open" in place of a type.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-f list	Read the names to examine from list, one per line, instead
	of from the command line.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
This is v7's file, and it prints v7's answer for a type.  The file
that shipped on the 1.6 standalone was a different program with a
different vocabulary - it said "C source file" where this says
"c program text", "Text file" where this says "ascii text" or
"English text", and "Relocatable module" where this says
"executable" - and it knew two source languages this one does not,
A-natural and CP/M text.  Its source is not in the distribution and
its detectors cannot be recovered from the binary, so nothing here
guesses at them.
.sp
The executable test is the machine's own.  v7 looked for the PDP-11
a.out magics 0407, 0410 and 0411; nothing on micronix has an a.out
header, so the Whitesmiths object magic is tested instead - see
obj.h.  The two archive magics are the same numbers on both machines
and are unchanged - see ar (1).
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
